          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between elevation and facade in house design?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Elevation technically refers to a two-dimensional drawing or view of one side of a building, while facade refers to the actual built exterior skin. In everyday use, homeowners searching for house elevation design in India are almost always referring to the front facade, since that is the side that gets the most design attention, budget and photography. Both terms are used interchangeably in practice, and either is understood by architects and facade companies."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does house elevation design cost in India in 2026?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Cost depends heavily on material and how much of the facade is treated versus left as plain plaster. A basic texture paint and plaster elevation can start around ₹150 to ₹300 per square foot. Stone cladding typically runs ₹350 to ₹900 per square foot. Engineered metal facade systems, including substructure, waterproofing and a factory finish, generally range from ₹800 to ₹2,500 per square foot depending on the system and pattern complexity. Many homeowners keep costs manageable by using a durable plaster base and reserving metal or parametric cladding for the entrance or a single feature wall."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which elevation style is trending in Indian homes right now?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Warm minimalism — clean geometric massing combined with warmer materials such as fluted metal panels, stone or wood-look cladding instead of stark white render — has become the dominant premium residential elevation style in India over the last two years. It photographs well, avoids the cold, clinical look of pure minimalism, and pairs easily with metal accent systems at the entrance or on feature walls."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does the elevation design need to change based on the city or climate?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. A house elevation design that performs well in Gurugram will not necessarily perform the same way in Chennai or Mumbai. Hot, dry regions need finishes that reflect heat rather than absorb it, such as PVDF-coated aluminium. Coastal cities need higher-grade, corrosion-resistant materials and careful joint detailing because of salt-laden air. High-rainfall regions depend more on drainage detailing and waterproofing behind the finish than on the surface material itself."
          }
        }
